% Walter Jekyll (ed.), Jamaican Song and Story (1907): ring games and dancing tunes from Jamaican singers.
% Published early from the workshop on 11 November 2026: each tune names what is guessed or left out in its N: lines,
% and its workshop record (or review item) is kept for further correction.

X:1
T:Galloway road
T:Jamaican ring game
C:traditional (Jamaica)
O:Jamaica
R:ring game
S:Walter Jekyll (ed.), Jamaican Song and Story (London: David Nutt, 1907), Project Gutenberg #35410 (music as MusicXML), no. XCVIII, p. 199
N:A Jamaican ring game in Jamaican Patois, as Jekyll printed it in 1907 (Allegretto): "I go to Galloway road (where there is a quarry). Girls and boys are breaking stones. They break them one by one, two by two... Choosing stones suggests choosing partners." Breaking stone by hand for the roads was hard, poorly paid labour in Jamaica then, worth a sentence of history in class. Jekyll describes choosing partners; schools often play its relative "Go Dung a Manuel Road" as a stone- or bean-bag-passing game on the beat, which suits this song too (a later practice, not Jekyll's). Jekyll was an English collector in colonial Jamaica; credit the song to the Jamaican singers he heard. The mento band is ours, in the style of Jamaican country bands.
M:2/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=100
