X:4
T:Little Sally Water
T:Jamaican ring game
C:traditional (Jamaica, from an English singing game)
O:Jamaica
R:ring game
S:Walter Jekyll (ed.), Jamaican Song and Story (London: David Nutt, 1907), Project Gutenberg #35410 (music as MusicXML), no. LXXXIX, p. 191
N:The ring game that gave its name to "playing in the ring", an English singing game sung in Jamaican style, with words and tune as Jekyll printed them: Andante, then Allegro where the pair wheel. The class joins hands in a ring; one child crouches in the middle as Sally Water, rises slowly at "Rise, Sally, rise", brushing away pretend tears, turns one way and another, and chooses a partner; the two wheel, and the partner becomes the next Sally. Jekyll's last verse, a mock wedding that ends with a kiss, is left out here, as many classes leave it out; the game is complete without it. Nothing in the tune is guessed.
M:2/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=84
K:Eb
